<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Your Course Grade: the dropped-midterm rule, a worked example, and a
  calculator (assets/grade-calculator.html, in an interactive iframe).

  Instructor edition only for now (Fall 2026): this file and the calculator
  are not in the student book, M13-Skeletal-Notes. The xi:include sits at
  the end of exercises.ptx so that every other worksheet keeps the number
  it has in the student book. To post it to students, copy both files there
  and add the same xi:include in that book's exercises.ptx.

  Rule: three midterms of 20 points, final 30, assignments 10 (syllabus
  Table 3.1). The lowest midterm is dropped and the best two, out of 40,
  are scaled to the full 60 midterm points. The calculator is marked
  component="web" like the book's other interactives, so the PDF keeps the
  rule and the example and points to the web page for the calculator.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-grade-calculator" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Your Course Grade</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Your lowest midterm exam score is dropped. Your best two midterms, out of
<m>40</m> points, are scaled to the full <m>60</m> midterm points, so the
midterms still count for <m>60\%</m> of your course grade:
<md>
\text{Midterm points} = \frac{\text{sum of your best two midterms}}{40}\times 60.
</md>
The final exam (<m>30</m> points) and the assignments (<m>10</m> points) are
unchanged, and your total out of <m>100</m> becomes a letter grade with the
percentage ranges in the syllabus.
</p>
</introduction>

<table xml:id="table-grade-drop-example">
<title>An example: from a C+ to a B+</title>
<tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major">
  <col/>
  <col halign="right"/>
  <col halign="right"/>
  <col halign="right"/>
  <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
    <cell>Component</cell><cell>Score</cell><cell>Without the drop</cell><cell>With the drop</cell>
  </row>
  <row><cell>Exam #1</cell><cell><m>8/20</m></cell><cell><m>8</m></cell><cell>dropped</cell></row>
  <row><cell>Exam #2</cell><cell><m>18/20</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell></row>
  <row><cell>Exam #3</cell><cell><m>18/20</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell><cell><m>18</m></cell></row>
  <row><cell>Midterm points (out of 60)</cell><cell/><cell><m>8 + 18 + 18 = 44</m></cell><cell><m>\frac{18 + 18}{40}\times 60 = 54</m></cell></row>
  <row><cell>Final Exam</cell><cell><m>25/30</m></cell><cell><m>25</m></cell><cell><m>25</m></cell></row>
  <row bottom="minor"><cell>Assignments</cell><cell><m>9/10</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell></row>
  <row><cell>Total (out of 100)</cell><cell/><cell><m>78</m> (C+)</cell><cell><m>88</m> (B+)</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
The best two midterms come to <m>36</m> out of <m>40</m>, which is
<m>90\%</m>, and that <m>90\%</m> counts for all <m>60</m> midterm points. The
weak first exam simply stops counting.
</p>

<sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
<figure xml:id="figure-grade-calculator">
  <caption>
    Enter your scores to see your course grade with the lowest midterm
    dropped. Leave a box empty for an exam you have not taken yet: empty boxes
    count as <m>0</m>, so the grade shown is the lowest the scores you entered
    can give, and while the final is still to come the calculator lists the
    final exam score each letter grade needs.
  </caption>
  <interactive xml:id="interactive-grade-calculator"
               iframe="grade-calculator.html"
               width="100%" aspect="15:16"/>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

<p component="print">
The web version of this page has a calculator: enter your scores and it shows
your course grade with the lowest midterm dropped.
</p>

</worksheet>
